{# =========================================================================
   Warp example — "check your email" confirmation
   =========================================================================
   Neutral landing page shown after the unified request form (login.twig) posts
   with the magic-link channel. Because the request endpoint is enumeration-safe,
   this page must read the same whether the address belongs to an existing
   account, a brand-new sign-up, or nothing at all — never confirm or deny that
   an account exists. The copy below covers all three cases.

   Reached via:  the hashed "redirect" the login form posts. Warp fixes the
   action route, not this page URL — this template lives wherever you copy it.

   This is a copy-in EXAMPLE. Drop it into your project's templates/, wrap it in
   your site layout, and restyle. Class hooks are low-specificity "warp-" names.
   ========================================================================= #}

{# Craft resolves {% include %} from the templates root. Leave warpBase empty
   for a root copy; set it to your subfolder (with trailing slash) if you nest
   the bundle, e.g. 'members/'. #}
{%- set warpBase = '' -%}
{%- set loginUrl = '/members/login' -%}   {# back to the request form — your route #}

<main class="warp-auth" id="main-content" tabindex="-1">
    <h1 class="warp-auth__title">{{ 'Check your email'|t('warp') }}</h1>

    {% include warpBase ~ '_partials/flashes' only %}

    <p class="warp-auth__intro">
        {{ 'If an account or a new registration matches that address, we have sent you an email with a secure sign-in link. Open it on this device and click the link to continue.'|t('warp') }}
    </p>

    <p class="warp-auth__hint">
        {{ 'The link expires shortly and can be used once. If it does not arrive within a few minutes, check your spam folder or request a new one.'|t('warp') }}
    </p>

    <p class="warp-auth__actions">
        <a class="warp-link" href="{{ url(loginUrl) }}">{{ 'Back to sign in'|t('warp') }}</a>
    </p>
</main>
